# Secondary Education Taste Considerations & Philosophical Probes
#
# Taste, aesthetics, developmental ethics, and wonder probes in adolescent learning and school design.

#>spw_cache_sec_taste
#:cache #!secondary_ed
#:layer #!pragmatics

^"intent"{
  ~#goal: "Synthesize the aesthetic, ethical, and developmental principles that distinguish genuine secondary intellectual flourishing from credentialist pressure-cookers."
  ~#resonance: "Adolescent agency, joy of inquiry, restorative hospitality, and anti-cynical pedagogical leadership"
}

^["the_tension_of_acceleration"]{
  critique: "The prevailing pathology of top American secondary schools is the conversion of youthful wonder into an anxiety-fueled credential accumulation race."
  antidote: "Cultivating academic rigor as an intrinsic joy—where a student solves an organic chemistry mechanism or analyzes a Toni Morrison passage out of sheer reverence for beauty rather than GPA optimization."
  distinctions: #[
    "Test-prep drill vs. Epistemic discovery: Memorizing formulas to beat an exam vs. deriving principles to understand the cosmos",
    "Performative résumé-stacking vs. Deep singular obsession: Joining ten clubs for college applications vs. spending two years restoring a native wetland",
    "Surveillance grading vs. Formative feedback: Weaponizing gradebooks as behavioral levers vs. treating drafts as living conversations"
  ]
}

^["aesthetic_and_spatial_architecture"]{
  spatial_critique: "Cinderblock corridors, barred windows, and ringing bells replicate the sensory architecture of prisons, inducing constant low-grade cortisol elevation."
  regenerative_spaces: #[
    "Natural light, courtyards, and communal green spaces that invite breathing and contemplation",
    "Open studio spaces and makerspaces where messy experimentation is celebrated over sterile compliance",
    "Acoustically dampened reading nooks and library sanctuaries where students can sink into uninterrupted solitude"
  ]
}

^["developmental_ethics_and_liminality"]{
  adolescent_condition: "Adolescence is not an incomplete state of adulthood to be hurried through, but a profound liminal metamorphosis where the moral and aesthetic self is born."
  ethical_imperatives: #[
    "The Right to Fail Safely: An educational environment where a catastrophic mistake in an experiment or essay does not destroy a young person's future",
    "Unconditional Regard: Mentorship that remains steadfast even when an adolescent is prickly, erratic, or struggling with turbulent emotions",
    "Egalitarian Rigor: Offering advanced intellectual invitations to all students rather than reserving depth exclusively for a pre-sorted elite"
  ]
}

^"taste"{
  ~#taste: "Secondary education reaches its peak when a seventeen-year-old realizes that their own mind is capable of creating original meaning in the world."
  ~#wonder: "Can a high school cultivate both the intellectual sharpness required for world-class scholarship and the gentle tenderness required to heal a fractured human spirit?"
}

^"invariants"{
  "Academic rigor must never be purchased at the price of student mental health and physical vitality.",
  "Every student, regardless of background or neurotype, possesses an innate right to high-level intellectual culture.",
  "School discipline must repair relationships and build community rather than exile or humiliate.",
}

#>wonder_sec_taste_1
?["What architectural conditions transform a high school from a high-stress holding facility into an intellectual commons?"]{
  #:depth #!aesthetic ~#lens(spatial_psychology)
  ~#hypothesis: "Removing bells, introducing biophilic natural lighting, and replacing double-loaded lockers with open collaborative commons lowers baseline adolescent cortisol by measurable margins while boosting collaborative cross-grade projects."
  !probe{ "Conduct comparative salivary cortisol and collaborative engagement mapping across traditional cell-and-bell high schools vs. open-campus biophilic designs." }
  $%[biometrics.cortisol_slope, collaboration.spontaneous_study_hours]
}

#>wonder_sec_taste_2
?["Can a high school dismantle the GPA hierarchy while simultaneously increasing students' depth of mastery and university readiness?"]{
  #:depth #!philosophical ~#lens(epistemic_agency)
  ~#hypothesis: "Transitioning to mastery-based transcripts and comprehensive oral defense of capstone portfolios produces graduates with higher college retention, deeper critical thinking, and superior emotional resilience than conventional letter-graded sorting."
  !probe{ "Track 10-year post-secondary outcomes for graduates of Mastery Transcript Consortium (MTC) schools vs. traditional AP-ranked high schools." }
  $%[retention.college_ten_year_success, agency.intellectual_initiative_score]
}
